---
/**
 * ui/version-switch — a frame is loaded when its panel is shown, and not before.
 *
 * ── THE FOURTH JAVASCRIPT EXCEPTION, AND WHY IT WAS TAKEN ────────────────────
 *
 * AGENTS.md allowed application JavaScript on exactly three things — the Helix
 * island, `ui/carousel` and `ui/embed-consent` — and said in as many words that
 * "a fourth exception is a decision to be taken, not a precedent to follow".
 * This is that fourth exception, taken by the owner on 2026-08-13, and the
 * decision record is `.agents/decisions/0007-version-switch-loads-frames-late.md`.
 *
 * WHAT FORCED IT. `patterns/VersionSwitch` shows one panel and hides the other
 * with `display: none`. A hidden element has no layout, so an iframe inside it
 * loads into a viewport of 0×0 — and `play.maar.world` sizes its p5 canvas once,
 * from the window it finds at startup. Version one's two players were therefore
 * booting at zero width, behind a closed panel, and were still zero-width when
 * the panel opened: the owner's report was that the code "is not loading
 * correctly". Nothing in this repository can reach inside a cross-origin frame
 * to resize it afterwards, and no CSS makes a `display: none` subtree lay out.
 *
 * THE ALTERNATIVES WERE TRIED FIRST, AND EACH ONE FAILS SOMEWHERE:
 *
 *   `loading="lazy"`          not a promise. Engines disagree about a lazy frame
 *                             inside `display: none`, and the ones that defer it
 *                             give no signal about when they stop deferring.
 *   `visibility: hidden`      keeps layout, so the frame WOULD size correctly —
 *                             and both panels then occupy space at once. Stacked
 *                             in one grid cell the page grows to the taller of
 *                             the two and leaves a screen of nothing under the
 *                             shorter one.
 *   fix it in the player      the real fix, and it is in another repository. The
 *                             note in styles/prose.css about the 14px scrollbar
 *                             says the same thing about the same app.
 *
 * ── WHAT IT DOES: PARK AND WAKE ──────────────────────────────────────────────
 *
 * Every frame ships in the HTML with its real `src`, which is what keeps this an
 * enhancement rather than a dependency. On load, the frames in the CLOSED panel
 * are parked — their address moved to `data-parked-src` and `about:blank` put in
 * its place — and on every switch the pair is reversed: the panel being left is
 * parked, the panel being opened is woken.
 *
 * Waking assigns the original address back, so a frame loads fresh, while
 * visible, at its real size. That is the owner's instruction almost word for
 * word: *"when we change to version 1, in that moment we load the iframes of the
 * version 1, not before"*.
 *
 * PARKING THE PANEL YOU LEAVE IS NOT HOUSEKEEPING. Both versions make sound. A
 * player left running behind a closed panel goes on playing into a page that is
 * now showing something else, and there is no control on screen to stop it —
 * `about:blank` is the stop button the visitor would otherwise not have.
 *
 * ── WITH THE SCRIPT OFF, NOTHING IS LOST THAT WAS NOT ALREADY LOST ───────────
 *
 * No JavaScript means no parking: every frame keeps the `src` the build wrote
 * and loads exactly as it did before this file existed. Version one is then
 * mis-sized behind its panel — today's bug, unchanged — and version two, which
 * is the panel the page opens on, is correct either way. The page loses no
 * content, no heading and no link, which is the test every other script here is
 * held to.
 *
 * NOTHING IS PERSISTED. No cookie, no storage. A reload starts over on version
 * two, the same way `ui/embed-consent` returns every embed to its poster.
 */
---

<script>
  for (const group of document.querySelectorAll<HTMLElement>('[data-version-switch]')) {
    const inputs = [...group.querySelectorAll<HTMLInputElement>('.version-switch__input')];
    const panels = [...group.querySelectorAll<HTMLElement>('.version-switch__panel')];
    /* One input drives one panel, by position — the same correspondence
       styles/version-switch.css draws with `:nth-of-type`. A group whose two
       lists disagree is a markup change that broke the pairing, and doing
       nothing is the honest response: every frame then keeps the src the build
       wrote, which is the no-script behaviour described above. */
    if (!inputs.length || inputs.length !== panels.length) continue;

    const park = (panel: HTMLElement) => {
      for (const frame of panel.querySelectorAll('iframe')) {
        const src = frame.getAttribute('src');
        if (!src || src === 'about:blank') continue;
        frame.dataset.parkedSrc = src;
        /* `setAttribute` rather than `frame.src = …`, so what goes back on the
           wake is the exact string the build wrote — not a resolved absolute
           URL, which is the same reason ui/embed-consent reads the attribute. */
        frame.setAttribute('src', 'about:blank');
      }
    };

    const wake = (panel: HTMLElement) => {
      /* Read a layout property first. The `change` event fires after `:checked`
         has flipped but style and layout are still stale, and the whole point of
         waking here is that the frame gets its address once the panel it lives
         in has a size. This forces that recalculation before the src lands. */
      void panel.offsetHeight;
      for (const frame of panel.querySelectorAll('iframe')) {
        const src = frame.dataset.parkedSrc;
        if (!src) continue;
        delete frame.dataset.parkedSrc;
        frame.setAttribute('src', src);
      }
    };

    const sync = () => {
      inputs.forEach((input, i) => (input.checked ? wake : park)(panels[i]));
    };

    /* On load this parks the closed panel — cancelling a load that has begun at
       zero size — and leaves the open one exactly as the build wrote it. */
    sync();
    for (const input of inputs) input.addEventListener('change', sync);
  }
</script>
